{
  "dom_id": "step-q98334",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(0,3)=8,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{4{e}^{2x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}+y}},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}+y}}.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,3),$ estimate the value of $f(-0.05,2.9).$",
  "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,3)+∇f(0,3)\\cdot ⟨x-0,y-3⟩ \\\\  & =f(0,3)+⟨{f}_{x}(0,3),{f}_{y}(0,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-3⟩ \\\\  & =8+⟨{f}_{x}(0,3),{f}_{y}(0,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(0,3)$ and ${f}_{y}(0,3)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,3) & =\\frac{4{e}^{2(0)}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2(0)}+3}}=2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,3) & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2(0)}+3}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈8+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(-0.05,2.9)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-0.05$ and $y=2.9,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.05,2.9) & ≈8+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.05,2.9-3⟩ \\\\  & =8+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.05,-0.1⟩ \\\\  & =8-0.1-0.1 \\\\  & =7.8\\end{aligned}\n$$",
